---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../site.config';

const tiers = [
  {
    tag: 'Tier 0',
    title: 'Hardware integrity',
    cadence: 'Every inference',
    body: "A real cryptographic hash of the monitor's own weights, checked against a fingerprint issued from outside the device, never from its own past state. A mismatch quarantines the node and requests a verified resync. No local repair is attempted on a system that can no longer vouch for itself.",
  },
  {
    tag: 'Meta',
    title: 'Behavioral self-test',
    cadence: 'Every few seconds',
    body: 'Weight checks catch storage corruption but miss compute-path faults and logic bugs. Four independent checks, canary inputs, output-distribution sanity, jitter stability, and fleet-consistency, catch the failures a hash alone cannot.',
  },
  {
    tag: 'Tier 1',
    title: 'Model drift',
    cadence: 'Every inference, sustained',
    body: 'A sequential change-detection test on out-of-distribution signal, so a single noisy input never triggers a false alarm, only genuine, sustained drift does. This is what catches a model that was incompletely trained: coverage gaps in the original training data surface at deployment as sustained novelty, not a single bad frame, exactly the pattern this test is built to detect rather than dismiss as noise. A second check then tells "the environment changed, same categories" apart from "this is a genuinely new category the model never saw," since only the former is safe to learn from automatically, a true unknown routes to a human, never a silent auto-fine-tune.',
  },
  {
    tag: 'Integrity',
    title: 'Model audit',
    cadence: 'Optional',
    body: 'This is the only tier that ever touches the model being watched, and it is opt-in, never assumed. By default the audited model stays fully opaque: no weights, no gradients, no training data, ever. Where a deployment explicitly grants write access to its own checkpoint, the same weight-hash and repeatability canary extend to it too, closing the gap between "the output looks anomalous" and "the model itself is compromised." Nothing above this tier, detection, diagnosis, or the default healing path, requires that access to work.',
  },
];

const remedies = [
  {
    title: 'Verified resync',
    body: "A hardware or behavioral fault in the monitor is never repaired locally. The only remedy is a fresh, server-verified model, a node can't certify its own health against itself.",
  },
  {
    title: "Heal ourselves, always",
    body: "The monitor's own model retrains on real, buffered data, validated against a held-out split before it's ever trusted, and every accepted version joins a bounded rollback ladder. This never touches, and never needs to touch, the model being watched.",
  },
  {
    title: 'Heal the watched model, only if invited',
    body: "By default, confirmed drift in the watched model is met with caution, not surgery: an extended hold, a wider safety margin, an alert, none of it requires touching that model's weights. A deployment can explicitly grant write access to its own checkpoint, and only then does the same fine-tune, validate, and rollback loop extend to it too, a privilege of that specific integration, never a requirement of the SDK.",
  },
  {
    title: "Escalate, don't loop",
    body: 'A fault that keeps recurring after repeated automated fixes stops retrying and raises a human alert instead. Self-healing that never gives up is a liability, not a feature.',
  },
];

const inputSnippet = `# Any framework, any model family, the SDK only needs three things
# per inference: an embedding, a confidence signal, and a decision.

from rtai import TrustLayer

trust = TrustLayer.from_config("rtai.config.yaml")

# In your existing inference loop, wherever it lives:
output = my_model.predict(input_batch)

decision = trust.evaluate(
    embedding=output.embedding,      # feature vector / penultimate layer
    confidence=output.confidence,    # softmax, logit, or calibrated score
    prediction=output.prediction,    # class, box, control signal, forecast...
    sensors=telemetry.snapshot(),    # optional: IMU, GPS, timestamps, anything
)

if not decision.trusted:
    actuator.hold()                  # veto the action before it happens
    log.warn(decision.diagnosis)     # e.g. "tier1_sustained_drift"
`;

const wrapperSnippet = `# Not on Python, or the model runs in a different process/service?
# Wrap the same three inputs behind a small local endpoint, the SDK
# talks to itself over gRPC/REST identically either way.

POST /v1/evaluate
{
  "embedding": [0.0123, -0.881, ...],
  "confidence": 0.94,
  "prediction": {"class": "pedestrian", "box": [120,80,300,400]},
  "sensors": {"imu_ok": true, "gps_hdop": 1.2}
}

-> {
  "trusted": false,
  "reliability_score": 0.11,
  "diagnosis": "tier0_hash_mismatch",
  "action": "quarantine_and_resync"
}
`;
---

<BaseLayout title="Product" description="How the tiered self-healing architecture works.">
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 pt-20 pb-16 sm:pt-28 sm:pb-20 border-b border-ink-600">
    <p class="eyebrow">Product</p>
    <h1 class="mt-5 max-w-3xl font-display text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-semibold leading-tight">
      A monitor that audits its own trust, not just yours.
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-lg text-paper-300">
      {site.name} sits between any AI model and its consumers, whether that's a flight controller, a
      dashboard, or an API caller, reading only its outputs, never its weights or gradients, and runs a
      tiered self-healing pipeline that never assumes it is the one part of the system that can't fail.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-14 sm:py-16 border-b border-ink-600">
    <div class="panel border-amber-600/60 bg-amber-500/[0.05]">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-amber-400">The guarantee, stated plainly</p>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl font-display text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold text-paper-100 leading-snug">
        We never require your model's weights, gradients, or training data, for detection, for diagnosis,
        or by default, for healing either.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-sm text-paper-300">
        Three fields at inference time, an embedding, a confidence signal, and a decision, are all this
        system has ever needed from the model it watches. Write access to that model is a deployment
        choice you can grant later for a deeper healing mode, never a precondition for using this product.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-20 sm:py-28">
    <p class="eyebrow">Architecture</p>
    <h2 class="mt-4 max-w-xl font-display text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight">
      Four tiers, each catching what the one before it structurally can't.
    </h2>

    <div class="mt-14 space-y-6">
      {
        tiers.map((t) => (
          <div class="panel grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-[140px_1fr_160px]">
            <div>
              <span class="status-chip ok">{t.tag}</span>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold">{t.title}</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-paper-300">{t.body}</p>
            </div>
            <div class="font-mono text-xs text-paper-500 sm:text-right">{t.cadence}</div>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-y border-ink-600 bg-ink-900/40">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-20 sm:py-28">
      <p class="eyebrow">Two different faults, two different tells</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 max-w-2xl font-display text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight">
        A hardware fault and a behavioral fault look identical from the outside. They aren't.
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="panel">
          <span class="status-chip ok">Hardware fault</span>
          <h3 class="mt-3 font-display text-base font-semibold">Something is wrong with what's stored</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-paper-300">
            A bit-flip, memory corruption, a value silently pushed out of range: the weights on disk are no
            longer the weights that were verified. A cryptographic hash catches this by definition,
            comparing what's stored now against a trusted fingerprint, byte for byte.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <span class="status-chip ok">Behavioral fault</span>
          <h3 class="mt-3 font-display text-base font-semibold">Something is wrong with what's computed</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-paper-300">
            The weights are completely intact and hash-verified, yet the same input stops producing the
            same output: a compute-path glitch, a transient processor error, a logic bug. A hash is
            structurally blind to this, there's nothing wrong with what's stored, so the behavioral suite
            instead re-runs known reference inputs and checks the output distribution itself for a stuck,
            NaN, or chaotically unstable signal, catching exactly what the hash can't.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-8 max-w-2xl text-sm text-paper-500">
        Both diagnoses currently route to the same remedy, a verified resync, since a fresh, externally-issued
        model is the available fix either way. If Meta keeps failing after repeated resyncs, the system stops
        assuming a reload will help and escalates to a human instead, see "Escalate, don't loop" below.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-b border-ink-600 bg-ink-900/40">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-20 sm:py-28">
      <p class="eyebrow">Fault localization</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 max-w-2xl font-display text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight">
        Detection and diagnosis stay separate from repair, on purpose.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-paper-300">
        Every signal above feeds a single routing table that decides where a problem actually originates
        before anything is touched: a compromised monitor is never trusted to grade the model it's
        watching, and a corrupted model is never mistaken for a legitimate discovery worth learning from.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-14 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">
        {
          remedies.map((r) => (
            <div class="panel">
              <h3 class="font-display text-base font-semibold">{r.title}</h3>
              <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-paper-300">{r.body}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-20 sm:py-28">
    <p class="eyebrow">Fleet learning</p>
    <div class="grid gap-10 md:grid-cols-[1fr_1fr] md:gap-16 items-start">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight">
        What one unit learns strengthens the fleet, without a single raw data point leaving it.
      </h2>
      <div class="space-y-5 text-paper-300">
        <p>
          A federated aggregation loop is also where a quarantined node's trust anchor actually comes
          from, never generated on-device. Updates are validated for sanity before they're accepted, and
          weighted by how reliable each contributing unit currently is, so a degraded node can't drag the
          whole fleet's model down while it's mid-recovery.
        </p>
        <p>
          The result: your fleet gets better from real operating conditions, not just from what was in
          the training set the day it shipped, and every step of that improvement is validated before
          it's trusted.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Integrate the SDK -->
  <section class="border-y border-ink-600 bg-ink-900/40">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-20 sm:py-28">
      <p class="eyebrow">Integrate the SDK</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 max-w-2xl font-display text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight">
        Three inputs. One decision object. No access to your model required.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-paper-300">
        The SDK doesn't care what kind of model you run, a vision transformer, a classical control loop, a
        forecasting model, or a policy network, only that it can produce these three things at inference
        time. If your model already logs a confidence score for debugging, you're most of the way there.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-12">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-paper-500 mb-3">Quickstart, Python</p>
        <pre class="panel overflow-x-auto text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-100 font-mono"><code>{inputSnippet}</code></pre>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-8">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-paper-500 mb-3">Any other stack, over a local service call</p>
        <pre class="panel overflow-x-auto text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-100 font-mono"><code>{wrapperSnippet}</code></pre>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-3">
        <div class="border-t border-ink-600 pt-5">
          <h3 class="font-display text-base font-semibold text-paper-100">Framework-agnostic</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-paper-500">PyTorch, TensorFlow, ONNX, a hand-rolled classical controller, it doesn't matter. Only the three output fields matter.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="border-t border-ink-600 pt-5">
          <h3 class="font-display text-base font-semibold text-paper-100">Language-agnostic</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-paper-500">Native SDK in Python today; a thin REST/gRPC wrapper makes it reachable from C++, Rust, Go, or anything else.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="border-t border-ink-600 pt-5">
          <h3 class="font-display text-base font-semibold text-paper-100">Deploy-target-agnostic</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-paper-500">Runs in-process on the same edge device as your model, as a sidecar container, or as a nearby local service.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-10">
        <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/integrate`} class="text-sm font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-teal-400 hover:text-teal-300">
          Full integration guide &rarr;
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-ink-600">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-20 sm:py-28 text-center">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-semibold max-w-xl mx-auto">
        Want the full technical breakdown, benchmarks included?
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-8">
        <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/technology`} class="btn-primary">Go to technology &amp; research</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

